        ["An ECG shows a PR interval of 0.26 seconds, and every P wave is followed by a QRS complex. What is this rhythm?",["First-degree AV block","Third-degree AV block","Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia"],"A PR interval over 0.20 s with 1:1 conduction is first-degree block, usually benign. Third-degree block has no relationship between P waves and QRS."],
        ["A regular rhythm strip shows R waves exactly 4 large boxes apart. What is the approximate heart rate?",["75 bpm","60 bpm","100 bpm","40 bpm"],"For regular rhythms, divide 300 by the number of large boxes between R waves: 300 ÷ 4 = 75 bpm."],
        ["Why does a very high heart rate increase the risk of myocardial ischemia?",["It raises O2 demand and shortens diastolic filling time","It lowers O2 demand but impairs the SA node","It increases coronary flow time during systole","It reduces cardiac output to the resting muscles"],"Coronary perfusion occurs mainly in diastole. Tachycardia increases demand while cutting the time for coronary filling."],
        ["A patient's monitor shows occasional unifocal PVCs at rest that do not increase during a low-level walk. He has no symptoms. What is the MOST appropriate action?",["Continue the activity with continued monitoring","Stop exercise immediately and call a code","Perform CPR and apply the AED pads","Discharge the patient from therapy for the day"],"Isolated unifocal PVCs that do not increase with activity are usually benign. Continue with monitoring and stop if they increase or become multifocal or couplets."],
        ["A patient on a beta-blocker had an exercise test while taking the medication. Which method is MOST appropriate for setting exercise intensity?",["A HR range from that test, checked against RPE","220 minus age, then multiplied by 85 percent","Resting HR plus 60 bpm regardless of symptoms","The HR at which the patient first begins sweating"],"Age-predicted formulas overestimate achievable HR on beta-blockers. A HR range from a test taken on the medication, plus RPE, is appropriate."],
        ["A patient with heart failure has a BNP that is much higher than at admission. What does this MOST likely indicate?",["Worsening heart failure with more ventricular stretch","Improvement in left ventricular systolic function","A pulmonary infection unrelated to the heart","Low hemoglobin from recent gastrointestinal bleeding"],"BNP is released in response to ventricular wall stretch and rises when HF worsens. A rising value warrants team discussion before exercise."]
